<section class="rc-s rc-ly"><div class="rc-w rc-ly-g"><div><p class="rc-lbl">Inside the shell</p><h2 class="rv">Three layers between you and the rain</h2><p class="rc-p">Most waterproof jackets are a sandwich. The outer face sheds water, a membrane blocks it, and a lining protects the membrane. &ldquo;2.5-layer&rdquo; and &ldquo;3-layer&rdquo; describe how they are bonded.</p></div><ol class="rc-ly-l stg"><li class="rv" style="--i:0"><span>L1</span><b>Face fabric</b><p>Tough nylon or polyester with a water-repellent finish so rain beads and rolls off.</p></li><li class="rv" style="--i:1"><span>L2</span><b>Membrane</b><p>A thin layer with pores too small for water drops but big enough for sweat vapour.</p></li><li class="rv" style="--i:2"><span>L3</span><b>Lining</b><p>A light inner layer or printed backer that protects the membrane from wear.</p></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="rc-s rc-rt" id="rating"><div class="rc-w rc-rt-g"><div class="rc-col"><div class="rc-tube" aria-hidden="true"><i id="rt-w"></i><span id="rt-mm">10,000 mm</span></div><div class="rc-tube-k" aria-hidden="true"><span>30k</span><span>20k</span><span>10k</span><span>5k</span><span>0</span></div></div>
<div><p class="rc-lbl">Tool one</p><h2 class="rv">Waterproof rating explainer</h2><p class="rc-p">Labels quote a &ldquo;hydrostatic head&rdquo; in millimetres: how tall a column of water the fabric can hold back before it leaks. Slide to see what each number means in real weather.</p>
<label class="rc-lb" for="rt-s">Hydrostatic head: <b id="rt-sv">10,000 mm</b></label><input type="range" id="rt-s" min="1000" max="30000" step="1000" value="10000" class="rc-rng">
